window.__hvBdRegister("arrian-anabasis", {"key":"arrian-anabasis","sections":[{"i":0,"j":12,"loc":"Preface; Book I 1–4","title":"Preface and the campaign to the Danube","summary":"Arrian explains that he follows Ptolemy and Aristobulus where they agree, choosing the more credible account where they differ, and treats other writers' stories as mere reports. Alexander, about twenty at Philip's death, is named leader of the Greeks against Persia by all but the Lacedaemonians, then marches north in spring 335 B.C. He forces Mount Haemus against Thracians who roll waggons down on the phalanx, defeats the Triballians at the river Lyginus, and reaches the Ister. Failing to land on the island of Peuce, he crosses the river by night on hide floats and dugout boats, routs the Getae and razes their town. Envoys come seeking friendship, including Celts who say they fear only that the sky may fall.","ideas":["Arrian trusts Ptolemy and Aristobulus because they served with Alexander and wrote after his death","The phalanx opens ranks or locks shields so the Thracian waggons roll over it","The night crossing of the Ister astonishes the Getae","The Celts' answer deflates Alexander's expectation of being feared"],"terms":[{"t":"phalanx","d":"The dense formation of Macedonian heavy infantry armed with long pikes."},{"t":"Agrianians","d":"Paeonian light troops who serve throughout as Alexander's picked skirmishers."}]},{"i":13,"j":18,"loc":"Book I 5–6","title":"War against Clitus and Glaucias in Illyria","summary":"Hearing that Clitus son of Bardylis has revolted with Glaucias king of the Taulantians, Alexander marches to Pelium, while his ally Langarus of the Agrianians keeps the Autariatians busy by raiding them. Clitus' men, after sacrificing three boys, three girls and three black rams, abandon the heights around Pelium. When Glaucias arrives, Alexander is caught in a narrow pass between river and mountain; he awes the enemy with rapid parade-ground drill, clears the ridges, and covers his river crossing with archers and war engines without losing a man. Three days later he crosses back by night and surprises the careless enemy camp; Clitus burns Pelium and flees to Glaucias.","ideas":["Langarus' loyalty is rewarded with honours and a promised royal marriage, but he dies","Drill manoeuvres used as a psychological weapon","Engines and archers cover a retreat across a river","A night attack on a camp without sentries or rampart"],"terms":[{"t":"hypaspists (shield-bearing guards)","d":"The elite infantry guard who accompany Alexander on his fastest and most dangerous operations."}]},{"i":19,"j":25,"loc":"Book I 7–10","title":"The revolt and destruction of Thebes","summary":"Theban exiles, claiming Alexander has died in Illyria, kill officers of the Macedonian garrison and rouse the city to revolt under the watchwords of liberty. Alexander reaches Boeotia by forced marches and camps near the city, still hoping for terms, but Ptolemy reports that Perdiccas attacked the stockade without orders, drawing the army into a battle that carried the Macedonians through the gates. Phocians, Plataeans and other Boeotians do most of the slaughter, and the allies decide to raze Thebes and sell its people, sparing priests, friends of Macedon, and Pindar's house. Arrian ranks the disaster above earlier Greek calamities and notes that many saw it as divine punishment for Thebes' past crimes. The other Greeks hasten to submit; Athens sends congratulations, refuses to hand over Demosthenes and the other orators, and Alexander relents, exiling only Charidemus.","ideas":["The false rumour of Alexander's death drives the revolt","Perdiccas' unordered assault decides the battle in Ptolemy's account","Arrian compares the fall of Thebes with Sicily, Aegospotami, Leuctra and Melos","Alexander pardons Athens, wanting no distrust behind him before Asia"],"terms":[{"t":"Cadmea","d":"The citadel of Thebes, held by a Macedonian garrison and blockaded by the rebels."},{"t":"Boeotarchs","d":"The chief magistrates of the Boeotian league, some of whom led the revolt."}]},{"i":26,"j":30,"loc":"Book I 11–12","title":"Crossing to Asia and the visit to Ilium","summary":"After festivals at Aegae and the omen of Orpheus' sweating statue, read by Aristander as promising poets much work, Alexander leaves Antipater in charge and marches to the Hellespont in spring 334 B.C. with about 30,000 foot and 5,000 horse. He sacrifices at the tomb of Protesilaus, steers his own ship across, and at Ilium dedicates his armour to Athena, takes sacred arms from the Trojan war, and garlands Achilles' tomb. Arrian breaks off to lament that Alexander, unlike Achilles, never found a worthy herald, and declares that writing this history has been his country, family and offices. Meanwhile the Persian satraps at Zeleia reject Memnon's advice to burn the country and starve Alexander out.","ideas":["Alexander re-enacts the Trojan war crossing through sacrifice and ritual","Arrian's second preface: Alexander's deeds lack a Homer or Xenophon","Memnon's scorched-earth plan is refused by Arsites out of pride and suspicion"],"terms":[{"t":"Companions (hetairoi)","d":"Alexander's noble cavalry and personal associates, the core of his striking force."}]},{"i":31,"j":38,"loc":"Book I 13–16","title":"The battle of the Granicus","summary":"Parmenio urges a dawn crossing, but Alexander refuses to let a mere brook halt him after the Hellespont and attacks at once. The armies are listed: the Persians line the steep bank with about 20,000 cavalry, the Greek mercenaries behind. Amyntas' advance guard suffers in the river, but Alexander drives in on the right, and the struggle on the bank becomes like an infantry fight. His spear breaks; he kills Mithridates and Rhoesaces, and Clitus son of Dropidas cuts off Spithridates' arm as he strikes at the king. The Persian horse break, the Greek mercenaries are cut down or captured, and Alexander honours the dead, visits the wounded, and sends 300 Persian panoplies to Athens.","ideas":["The exchange between cautious Parmenio and impatient Alexander","Cornel-wood lances prevail over Persian javelins","Clitus saves the king's life","Greek mercenaries captured are sent to labour in Macedonia as traitors to Greece"],"terms":[{"t":"Lysippus","d":"The sculptor commissioned to make bronze statues of the fallen Companions at Dium."}]},{"i":39,"j":45,"loc":"Book I 17–19","title":"Sardis, Ephesus and the siege of Miletus","summary":"Alexander appoints Calas satrap, receives the surrender of Sardis and grants the Lydians their old laws; a thunderstorm marks the site for a temple of Zeus. At Ephesus he restores democracy, assigns the Persian tribute to Artemis, and checks the people's revenge on the oligarchs. His fleet under Nicanor seizes Lade before the larger Persian fleet arrives, and Alexander rejects Parmenio's call for a sea battle, reading the eagle seen on land as meaning victory over the fleet from the land. He storms Miletus while his ships block the harbour, spares 300 brave mercenaries on an islet, and drives off the Persian fleet by denying it water.","ideas":["Democracies replace oligarchies in the Greek cities","Alexander restrains the Ephesian mob from killing the innocent","Two readings of the eagle omen","Land forces used to defeat a fleet by denying it harbours and water"],"terms":[{"t":"satrap (viceroy)","d":"A Persian provincial governor; Alexander keeps the office and fills it with his own men."}]},{"i":46,"j":51,"loc":"Book I 20–23","title":"The siege of Halicarnassus","summary":"Alexander disbands his fleet, judging it too weak and too costly, and moves into Caria against Halicarnassus, defended by Memnon, now Darius' commander of the coast and fleet. A night attempt on Myndus fails; he fills the ditch, brings up towers and rams, and beats off repeated sorties. Two drunken soldiers of Perdiccas' brigade start a fight at the wall that almost takes the city; the defenders build an inner crescent wall, and a mass sortie ends in heavy slaughter at a collapsed bridge and shut gates. Memnon and Orontobates fire the city and withdraw to two citadels, which Alexander leaves under guard, razing the city and making Ada, who had adopted him as her son, ruler of Caria.","ideas":["The decision to disband the fleet and win the sea from the land","An accidental assault caused by two boastful messmates","Alexander holds back his army hoping Halicarnassus will surrender","Queen Ada adopts Alexander and receives Caria"],"terms":[{"t":"Ada","d":"Carian princess, displaced by Pixodarus, whom Alexander restores as ruler of all Caria."}]},{"i":52,"j":61,"loc":"Book I 24–29","title":"Lycia, Pamphylia, Pisidia and the march to Gordium","summary":"Alexander sends the newly married men home for the winter and Parmenio to Sardis, then takes the coast of Lycia and Pamphylia to deny ports to the Persian fleet. Near Phaselis he learns that Alexander son of Aëropus, commander of the Thessalian horse, has been offered the throne of Macedonia by Darius; a captured Persian envoy and a swallow omen read by Aristander confirm it, and he is arrested. A shift to a north wind lets the army pass along the beach to Perga. Aspendus breaks its agreement and pays a harsher one; Alexander forces the pass at Termessus, defeats the Pisidians at Sagalassus, and receives Celaenae on terms. At Gordium the reinforcements join him, and he refuses an Athenian plea to free the Granicus prisoners until the war is won.","ideas":["Coastal conquest as the answer to Persian sea power","The plot of Alexander son of Aëropus and the swallow omen","The Sidetans' legend of their unique language","Fear is kept up among the Greeks by holding the Athenian prisoners"],"terms":[{"t":"Aristander of Telmissus","d":"Alexander's soothsayer, who interprets the omens throughout the campaign."}]},{"i":62,"j":70,"loc":"Book II 1–5","title":"Memnon's war at sea and the Gordian knot","summary":"Memnon takes Chios and Lesbos and besieges Mitylene but dies; Pharnabazus and Autophradates force the city to renounce its treaty with Alexander and impose a tyrant, while Proteas captures eight Persian ships at Siphnus. At Gordium Arrian tells how the poor farmer Gordius' wagon brought Midas to the Phrygian throne, and how whoever loosed its cord would rule Asia; Alexander either cut the knot or, as Aristobulus says, drew out the pole-pin, and Arrian will not affirm which. Alexander passes the Cilician Gates unopposed and saves Tarsus, then falls gravely ill, perhaps after bathing in the Cydnus; he drinks Philip the physician's draught while handing him Parmenio's letter accusing him of poison. He recovers and campaigns through Cilicia, noting the monument of Sardanapalus at Anchialus.","ideas":["Memnon's death injures the Persian plan to carry the war into Greece","Two versions of the Gordian knot, left undecided","Alexander's trust in Philip the physician","The Sardanapalus inscription: eat, drink and play"],"terms":[{"t":"Peace of Antalcidas","d":"The King's Peace of 387/6 B.C., whose terms the Persians reimpose on the islands."}]},{"i":71,"j":76,"loc":"Book II 6–9","title":"Darius advances and the armies form at Issus","summary":"Darius, encamped on an open plain at Sochi, ignores the deserter Amyntas' advice to stay where his numbers count; flattered by courtiers who think Alexander is afraid, and led by fate, he crosses the mountains and comes behind Alexander at Issus, mutilating the sick left there. Alexander confirms the report by ship and addresses his officers, contrasting free, hardened Macedonians with enervated subjects and paid mercenaries, and recalling Xenophon's Ten Thousand. He retakes the pass by night and deploys by the sea, with Parmenio holding the left. Darius screens his deployment with cavalry beyond the Pinarus, sets 30,000 Greek mercenaries and Cardaces against the phalanx, and masses his horse by the sea; Alexander shifts the Thessalians and extends his right.","ideas":["Bad advice prevails at Darius' court because it is pleasant","Arrian sees fate transferring Asia from Persians to Macedonians","Alexander's speech before Issus","The narrow ground neutralises Persian numbers"],"terms":[{"t":"Cardaces","d":"Persian heavy infantry posted on either side of Darius' Greek mercenaries."}]},{"i":77,"j":82,"loc":"Book II 10–12","title":"The battle of Issus and the captive queens","summary":"Darius waits behind the river bank and a stockade, which Alexander's men take as a sign of fear. Alexander charges through the river on the right and routs the Persian left, but the phalanx centre is broken on the steep banks and the Greek mercenaries press it hard until the right wing wheels onto their flank; Ptolemy son of Seleucus falls. Darius flees as soon as his left is broken, abandoning chariot, shield, mantle and bow; Persian losses are put at 100,000, and his mother, wife and children are taken in the camp. Alexander, wounded in the thigh, buries the dead and rewards the brave, and assures the women of their royal status; Arrian reports, without vouching for it, the story of Darius' mother bowing to Hephaestion.","ideas":["The struggle at the broken phalanx between Greek mercenaries and Macedonians","Darius' early flight decides the battle","Alexander claims to fight for the empire of Asia, not from hatred","Arrian praises Alexander even if the Hephaestion story is only likely"],"terms":[]},{"i":83,"j":91,"loc":"Book II 13–16","title":"Letters with Darius and the Tyrian refusal","summary":"Darius flees toward the Euphrates while Greek deserters escape by sea to Cyprus and Egypt, and the Persian admirals confer with Agis of Sparta in the islands. Strato of Aradus surrenders his realm, and at Marathus Darius writes asking for his family and an alliance; Alexander answers with a list of Persian wrongs and demands to be addressed as lord of all Asia, offering another battle otherwise. He releases the captured Theban envoys and honours Iphicrates the Athenian. Byblus and Sidon submit, and Tyre offers obedience, but when Alexander asks to sacrifice to Heracles the Tyrians refuse to admit any Persian or Macedonian. Arrian digresses on the Tyrian Heracles, older than the Argive hero, and rationalises the story of Geryon.","ideas":["Alexander's letter claims Asia by right of victory and the gods' gift","Mercy to Thebans and honour to Athenians among the captured envoys","Tyre tries to stay neutral while the war is undecided","Arrian distinguishes several figures called Heracles"],"terms":[{"t":"Tyrian Heracles","d":"Arrian calls him the Tyrian Heracles, the god of the most ancient temple in Tyre, distinct from Alcmena's son."}]},{"i":92,"j":97,"loc":"Book II 17–20","title":"The case for Tyre and the mole","summary":"Alexander tells his council that he cannot march on Egypt or Babylon with Tyre and the Persian fleet behind him; taking Tyre would bring over the Phoenician fleet and Cyprus and give him the sea. A dream of Heracles leading him into the city is read as a promise of capture with labour. The Macedonians build a mole toward the island city, but Tyrian triremes harass them and a fire ship burns the towers and engines. Alexander widens the mole and goes to Sidon, where the Phoenician kings, the Cypriots and others bring him over two hundred ships; after a raid into the Anti-Libanus he sails on Tyre, and the Tyrians block their harbours rather than fight.","ideas":["Strategic argument: control of the coast before pursuing Darius","The fire ship destroys the first siege works","The fall of Phoenician cities brings their fleets over to Alexander","Tyrian sea power is neutralised by numbers"],"terms":[{"t":"mole","d":"The causeway of stone and timber Alexander builds from the mainland to the island city."}]},{"i":98,"j":102,"loc":"Book II 21–24","title":"The storming of Tyre","summary":"Engines are mounted on ships, and the Macedonians haul away the stones blocking the walls, answering Tyrian divers who cut their cables by using chains. A surprise Tyrian sortie at midday sinks Cypriot quinqueremes, but Alexander returns early, blocks the harbour and catches most of the ships. After testing the walls he breaches the side facing Egypt; on a calm day bridges are thrown onto the breach, Admetus falls as first man on the wall, and Alexander leads the guards in, while the Phoenician and Cypriot fleets force both harbours. About 8,000 Tyrians are killed, angered Macedonians remembering murdered prisoners; those in the temple of Heracles, including king Azemilcus and Carthaginian envoys, are spared and some 30,000 sold. Alexander sacrifices to Heracles and dedicates the battering engine.","ideas":["An engineering contest of stones, cables, chains and armoured boats","The failed midday sortie against the Cypriots","Simultaneous assault on the breach and both harbours","Sanctuary in the temple of Heracles is respected"],"terms":[{"t":"quinquereme","d":"A large warship rowed with five files of oarsmen per unit, heavier than a trireme."}]},{"i":103,"j":106,"loc":"Book II 25–27","title":"Darius' second offer and the siege of Gaza","summary":"During the siege of Tyre, Darius offers 10,000 talents, all land west of the Euphrates and his daughter's hand; Parmenio says he would accept if he were Alexander, and Alexander replies that so would he, were he Parmenio, demanding the whole instead of a part. Darius prepares for war again. On the road to Egypt the eunuch Batis holds Gaza on its high mound; the engineers think it impregnable, so Alexander insists on taking it. A bird drops a stone on his head and Aristander warns of danger; Alexander, rushing to help men driven from the siege mound, is wounded through shield and breastplate. With the Tyre engines, mines and a vast mound the walls fall at the fourth assault, the defenders die at their posts, and the women and children are sold.","ideas":["The Parmenio exchange over Darius' terms","Difficulty itself becomes Alexander's reason to attack","Aristander's prophecy of capture and wound is fulfilled","Gaza is resettled as a fortress for the war"],"terms":[{"t":"talent","d":"A large unit of weight used for silver or gold reckoning, in which ransoms and fines are counted."}]},{"i":107,"j":114,"loc":"Book III 1–6","title":"Egypt, Alexandria, and the oracle of Ammon","summary":"Alexander marches from Gaza to Pelusium, and Mazaces, Darius's viceroy, hands Egypt over without a fight. Alexander sacrifices to Apis at Memphis and marks out the site of Alexandria, its boundaries traced with soldiers' barley, which Aristander reads as a sign of plenty. Hegelochus reports the recovery of Tenedos, Chios, Lesbos and Cos. Alexander crosses the desert to the oracle of Ammon, guided, according to Ptolemy, by two serpents, according to Aristobulus by two ravens, and hears what he wished. He then divides the government of Egypt among several officers and returns to Tyre, where he settles appointments and readies the march inland.","ideas":["Egypt surrenders and Alexandria is founded","The journey to Ammon is told through Ptolemy's and Aristobulus's differing accounts","Egypt's power is split among several officers so no one man holds it","Harpalus is forgiven his desertion and made treasurer again"],"terms":[{"t":"Ammon","d":"The Libyan god whose desert oracle Alexander consulted, from whom he also traced his descent."},{"t":"Aristander","d":"Alexander's soothsayer from Telmissus, whose readings of omens Arrian reports again and again."}]},{"i":115,"j":120,"loc":"Book III 7–11","title":"March to Gaugamela and the two battle lines","summary":"Alexander crosses the Euphrates at Thapsacus and then the Tigris, where an eclipse of the moon is read as promising victory within the month. Captured scouts tell him that Darius's huge army, under Bessus and many satraps, is camped on levelled ground at Gaugamela. After reconnoitring, Alexander tells his officers the battle will decide the rule of Asia. He rejects Parmenio's advice to attack by night, saying he will not steal a victory, and Arrian approves his reasons. Arrian then sets out the order of the Persian line, taken from Darius's captured battle plan, and the Macedonian order of Companions, phalanx brigades and Parmenio's left.","ideas":["The lunar eclipse is taken as an omen of victory","Alexander says the battle will decide who rules Asia","He refuses a night attack, and Arrian defends that choice","The two armies' dispositions are given in detail"],"terms":[{"t":"Companion cavalry (hetairoi)","d":"Alexander's elite Macedonian horse, posted on the right wing under Philotas."},{"t":"scythe-bearing chariots","d":"Persian chariots with blades fixed to them, meant to break the phalanx."}]},{"i":121,"j":124,"loc":"Book III 12–15","title":"The battle of Gaugamela (Arbela)","summary":"Alexander forms a second line in reserve and sets flank guards at an angle to meet an outflanking attack. During the battle he edges right, off the levelled ground. His light troops destroy or let through the scythed chariots, and when the Persian line opens a gap he drives a wedge of Companions and phalanx at Darius, who flees first. Persian and Indian horse break through to the baggage, and Parmenio, hard pressed on the left, calls for help. Alexander turns back and fights the fiercest cavalry action of the day, then pursues Darius to Arbela, which fulfils Aristander's prophecy.","ideas":["A reserve phalanx and angled flank guards guard against encirclement","Javelin-men neutralize the chariots","The charge into the gap puts Darius to flight","Parmenio's appeal recalls Alexander from the pursuit"],"terms":[{"t":"phalanx","d":"The dense formation of Macedonian infantry armed with long pikes."}]},{"i":125,"j":128,"loc":"Book III 16–18","title":"Babylon, Susa, the Persian Gates, Persepolis","summary":"Darius flees into Media, but Alexander marches to Babylon, which surrenders. There he orders the temples Xerxes destroyed rebuilt and sacrifices to Belus as the Chaldaeans direct. At Susa he takes 50,000 talents and sends the statues of Harmodius and Aristogeiton back to Athens. He crushes the mountain Uxians, who demanded a toll, and turns Ariobarzanes's wall at the Persian Gates by a night march round the defenders while Craterus attacks from the front. At Persepolis he burns the palace against Parmenio's advice, as revenge for the Persian invasion of Greece, an act Arrian judges imprudent.","ideas":["Babylon and Susa surrender with their treasures","The Uxians' toll is answered with force","The Persian Gates are taken by an outflanking march","The burning of Persepolis, which Arrian criticizes"],"terms":[{"t":"Persian Gates","d":"The mountain pass into Persis that Ariobarzanes walled off."},{"t":"Belus","d":"The chief god of Babylon, whose temple Alexander ordered rebuilt."}]},{"i":129,"j":134,"loc":"Book III 19–22","title":"Pursuit and death of Darius","summary":"Alexander advances into Media, where Darius has abandoned his plan to fight and fled from Ecbatana. At Ecbatana Alexander discharges the Thessalians and Greek allies with a bonus and leaves Harpalus and Parmenio with the treasure. A forced march brings him to Rhagae and through the Caspian Gates, and he learns that Bessus, Nabarzanes and Barsaëntes have seized Darius. Pressing on with mounted infantry by a waterless shortcut, he overtakes the fugitives. The conspirators wound Darius and leave him to die. Arrian sends him to a royal burial and sums up his reign as one disaster after another.","ideas":["Greek allies are sent home and the treasure secured at Ecbatana","Bessus and his fellow conspirators seize the king","A relentless pursuit with mounted infantry","Arrian's verdict on Darius as unwarlike but not tyrannical"],"terms":[{"t":"Caspian Gates","d":"The pass on the road to Parthia that Darius went through in his flight."}]},{"i":135,"j":140,"loc":"Book III 23–27","title":"Hyrcania, Areia, and the fall of Philotas","summary":"Alexander enters Hyrcania and receives the surrender of Darius's nobles and of Artabazus. He demands unconditional surrender from Darius's Greek mercenaries, and sorts the Greek envoys by whether they had broken the Greeks' common resolution. He subdues the Mardians and hears that Bessus has proclaimed himself King Artaxerxes. Satibarzanes's revolt in Areia draws him back by a forced march, and Barsaëntes is handed over and executed. In Drangiana the conspiracy of Philotas comes to light: Philotas is tried and killed, and Parmenio is put to death by order. Amyntas is acquitted, the Companion cavalry is split between Hephaestion and Clitus, and the Euergetae are honoured.","ideas":["Greek mercenaries who served Persia are treated as offenders against Greece","Bessus takes the royal title","Philotas is convicted for keeping silent about a plot, and Parmenio is killed","No single friend is to command all the Companion cavalry"],"terms":[{"t":"Euergetae","d":"The 'Benefactors', formerly Ariaspians, so named for helping Cyrus; Alexander left them free."}]},{"i":141,"j":147,"loc":"Book III 28–30","title":"Over the Caucasus to the Oxus and Tanais","summary":"Alexander subdues the Drangians, Gadrosians and Arachotians in deep snow, while his officers kill Satibarzanes in battle in Areia. He founds an Alexandria by Mount Caucasus, and Arrian records Aristobulus's notes on the range and its silphium. Bessus wastes the land, crosses the Oxus and is abandoned by the Bactrian cavalry. Alexander takes Bactra, ferries the army over the Oxus on stuffed hides, and sends Ptolemy, who seizes Bessus. Bessus is displayed bound and scourged, though Aristobulus tells the capture differently. At the Tanais (Jaxartes) Alexander storms a rocky stronghold and is shot through the leg.","ideas":["Satibarzanes falls in single combat with Erigyius","The Oxus is crossed on hide floats","Bessus is betrayed and shamed","Arrian holds that this Tanais differs from Herodotus's river"],"terms":[{"t":"silphium","d":"A valuable plant that grew on the Caucasus and was prized at Cyrene, much loved by sheep."},{"t":"Tanais / Jaxartes","d":"The river of Sogdiana that Arrian distinguishes from the European Tanais."}]},{"i":148,"j":153,"loc":"Book IV 1–5","title":"Sogdian revolt and the Scythians","summary":"Scythian envoys arrive, and Alexander sends Companions back with them to spy on Scythia while he plans a city on the Tanais. The Sogdianians, stirred up by Bessus's captors, revolt and kill their garrisons. Alexander storms five towns in two days and takes Cyropolis by creeping in along a dry river channel, being struck on the head and neck by a stone. Despite unfavourable sacrifices he crosses the Tanais under cover of his war engines and routs the Scythian horse by mixing light infantry with his cavalry, but bad water makes him ill, as Aristander had warned. Meanwhile Spitamenes destroys a Macedonian relief force at the river Polytimetus.","ideas":["The seven cities fall, Cyropolis through its riverbed","Alexander would face danger rather than be a laughing-stock to Scythians","Mixed cavalry and light troops defeat the Scythians' circling tactics","Disaster at the Polytimetus under divided command"],"terms":[{"t":"Spitamenes","d":"The Sogdian leader who betrayed Bessus and then led the resistance against Alexander."}]},{"i":154,"j":157,"loc":"Book IV 6–9","title":"Bessus punished and the killing of Clitus","summary":"Arrian gives Aristobulus's account of the Polytimetus disaster, which blames confusion over command. Alexander then drives Spitamenes from Maracanda and lays the country waste. At Zariaspa he has Bessus's nose and ears cut off. Arrian condemns this as barbaric and criticizes Alexander's taste for Median dress, saying no conquest brings happiness without self-control. He then tells how Clitus, angered by flatterers at a drinking party, belittled Alexander and praised Philip, and was killed by the king. Arrian censures Clitus but praises Alexander's remorse, and rebukes Anaxarchus for telling him that whatever a king does is just.","ideas":["Arrian criticizes Bessus's mutilation and Alexander's adoption of Median ways","Happiness requires self-control","Clitus is killed in a drunken quarrel","Anaxarchus's flattery is judged more harmful than the deed itself"],"terms":[{"t":"Anaxarchus","d":"A sophist at court who consoled Alexander by saying a king's acts are just."},{"t":"sōphrosynē (self-control)","d":"The restraint Arrian says Alexander's success could not stand in for."}]},{"i":158,"j":162,"loc":"Book IV 10–14","title":"Callisthenes, prostration, and the pages' plot","summary":"Callisthenes, Aristotle's pupil, objects to the proposal that Alexander be honoured as a god. When Anaxarchus urges prostration at a banquet, Callisthenes answers that honours due to gods and those due to men must be kept apart, and that Greeks, a free people, will not prostrate themselves. Alexander drops the subject for the Macedonians, and Callisthenes loses a kiss for refusing the bow. Arrian faults both Alexander's insolence and Callisthenes's tactlessness. The royal pages, led by Hermolaüs after a humiliating flogging, plot to kill Alexander but are foiled when a Syrian prophetess keeps him drinking. They are betrayed and executed, and the sources disagree about Callisthenes's guilt and death.","ideas":["Callisthenes's speech against prostration (proskynēsis)","Arrian blames both king and philosopher","The pages' conspiracy and its discovery","Ptolemy and Aristobulus disagree on how Callisthenes died"],"terms":[{"t":"proskynēsis (prostration)","d":"The Persian bow before the king, which Callisthenes says is an honour for gods only."},{"t":"royal pages","d":"Sons of Macedonian nobles who attended and guarded the king, as instituted by Philip."}]},{"i":163,"j":170,"loc":"Book IV 15–20","title":"End of Spitamenes and the Sogdian Rock","summary":"The European Scythians offer a royal bride, and Pharasmanes of Chorasmia offers to guide an expedition to the Euxine. Alexander declines both, saying that his mind is set on India. A spring of oil near the Oxus is read as a sign of labours ending in victory. Columns sweep Sogdiana, while Spitamenes raids Bactria with Massagetian horse until Craterus and then Coenus defeat him. The Massagetae send Alexander his head. In spring, after the defenders tell him to find winged soldiers, three hundred climbers scale the Sogdian Rock with tent pegs and it surrenders. Alexander marries the captive Roxana, and Arrian praises his restraint toward Darius's wife.","ideas":["Alexander turns down Scythian marriage and a Euxine campaign for now","Spitamenes is beheaded by his own allies","The 'winged men' take the Sogdian Rock","The marriage to Roxana and Darius's prayer that only Alexander succeed him"],"terms":[{"t":"Massagetae","d":"Nomadic Scythians who sheltered Spitamenes and finally killed him."},{"t":"Roxana","d":"Daughter of Oxyartes, captured on the Sogdian Rock and married by Alexander."}]},{"i":171,"j":175,"loc":"Book IV 21–25","title":"Rock of Chorienes and entry into India","summary":"Alexander bridges the ravine round the rock of Chorienes with stakes and hurdles until his arrows reach the top. Persuaded by Oxyartes, Chorienes surrenders, keeps his rule, and feeds the snowbound army. Craterus crushes the last rebels, and Alexander crosses the Caucasus toward India, sending Hephaestion and Perdiccas with Taxiles to bridge the Indus. Alexander himself campaigns among the Aspasians, is wounded in the shoulder, and razes a town. Ptolemy kills the Aspasian chief in single combat, and a three-column attack defeats the gathered tribes, taking many prisoners and oxen. The army then moves on against the Assacenians.","ideas":["The ravine is bridged and Chorienes yields willingly","Taxiles submits and the Indus crossing is prepared","Ptolemy kills the Aspasian chief in single combat","Arigaeum is refortified and resettled"],"terms":[{"t":"Taxiles","d":"An Indian ruler west of the Indus who came to Alexander with gifts and elephants."}]},{"i":176,"j":181,"loc":"Book IV 26–30","title":"Massaga, Bazira, Ora, and the Rock Aornus","summary":"At Massaga Alexander lures the defenders out with a feigned retreat, and then breaches the wall. When a siege bridge collapses he is repulsed. The city's ruler is killed and his Indian mercenaries surrender, but when they try to slip away by night Alexander cuts them down and storms the city. Ora falls at the first assault, and the people of Bazira flee to the Rock Aornus, which legend said Heracles could not take, a tale Arrian doubts. Ptolemy seizes a height by a hidden path, Alexander joins him, and the army builds a mound toward the rock. The defenders plan to slip away under a truce, but Alexander scales the abandoned part and catches them in flight. He then hunts elephants and builds boats for the Indus.","ideas":["A feigned retreat and a collapsed siege bridge at Massaga","The massacre of the departing mercenaries","Arrian is sceptical of the Heracles legend","The siege mound and the capture of Aornus"],"terms":[{"t":"Aornus","d":"A huge Indian rock fortress said to have defied Heracles, taken by Alexander."},{"t":"Abisares","d":"An Indian ruler who sent help to Ora and to whom fugitives fled."}]},{"i":182,"j":191,"loc":"Book V, 1–8","title":"Nysa, the Indus, and the march to Taxila","summary":"At Nysa the envoy Acuphis asks Alexander to leave the city free, since Dionysus founded it. Alexander grants this, partly because he wants the Dionysus story believed, and the Macedonians crown themselves with ivy on Mount Merus. Arrian declines to judge such legends, notes Eratosthenes' scepticism, and digresses on the Indus, the rivers and geography of India, and the Roman way of bridging rivers with boats. Alexander crosses the Indus, is welcomed by Taxiles at Taxila, has his boats cut up and carried to the Hydaspes, and marches against Porus.","ideas":["Alexander uses the Dionysus legend to spur his men to surpass the god","Acuphis talks Alexander out of taking a hundred of Nysa's best men","India is said to be largely a river-made alluvial plain","Arrian admits his sources do not say how the Indus was bridged"],"terms":[{"t":"Meros","d":"The mountain by Nysa, named, the Nysaeans said, from the thigh of Zeus in which Dionysus grew."},{"t":"Caucasus / Parapamisus","d":"Arrian keeps the Macedonian name Caucasus for the Parapamisus range, while noting it may have been given to flatter Alexander."}]},{"i":192,"j":197,"loc":"Book V, 9–14","title":"Crossing the Hydaspes in the storm","summary":"Porus holds the far bank of the swollen Hydaspes with elephants, and Alexander wears him down with feints, night alarms and talk of waiting for winter. He leaves Craterus at the main camp with orders to cross only if the elephants leave, posts Meleager, Attalus and Gorgias in between, and secretly takes a picked force to a wooded headland and island 150 stades upstream. In a night storm the army crosses on boats and hay-stuffed skins, only to find it has landed on a second island and must ford a deep channel. Arrian then sets out conflicting accounts of the son of Porus's arrival, preferring Ptolemy's.","ideas":["Repeated false alarms teach Porus to ignore noise","Horses would panic at the elephants, so the crossing must be stolen","The storm hides the crossing but swells the ford","Aristobulus, others and Ptolemy differ on the son of Porus and Bucephalas"],"terms":[{"t":"Enyalius","d":"A war god in whose honour the cavalry raised the battle-cry during the night feints."}]},{"i":198,"j":204,"loc":"Book V, 15–20","title":"The battle with Porus and its aftermath","summary":"Alexander's cavalry routs the son of Porus, who is killed, and Porus marches out with elephants screening his infantry. Alexander avoids the centre, attacks the Indian left with cavalry while Coenus takes them in the rear, and the trapped elephants trample friend and foe until the phalanx closes in; Craterus crosses to join the pursuit. Porus fights on until wounded, rejects Taxiles, and surrenders to his friend Meroës; asked how he wishes to be treated, he answers that he wants a kingly treatment, and Alexander restores and enlarges his realm. Alexander founds Nicaea and Bucephala, honours the dead horse Bucephalas, conquers the Glausians, and receives envoys from Abisares.","ideas":["Cavalry on both flanks breaks the Indian horse","Panicked elephants become as dangerous to the Indians as to the enemy","Porus, unlike Darius, does not flee first","Porus's request to be treated as a king wins him more land"],"terms":[{"t":"Bucephalas","d":"Alexander's horse, about thirty years old, who died of toil and age and gave his name to the city Bucephala."},{"t":"Nicaea","d":"The city founded on the battlefield, named for the victory."}]},{"i":205,"j":209,"loc":"Book V, 20–24","title":"Across the Acesines to the storming of Sangala","summary":"Alexander crosses the rocky, rapid Acesines with losses among the boats, sends Porus home to raise troops, and pursues the other, 'bad' Porus, who has fled. He crosses the Hydraotes and hears that the Cathaeans and other independent tribes are gathering at Sangala. The Cathaeans defend a hill behind a triple ring of waggons; Alexander leads the infantry on foot through the rows and blockades the city. Ptolemy blocks a night escape by the lake, the city is taken with heavy Indian losses and razed, and Alexander advances to the Hyphasis.","ideas":["Arrian reasons from the Acesines to the Indus's breadth","The second Porus fled from fear of his namesake","Waggon laager at Sangala is broken by the phalanx","Deserters reveal the planned escape by the lake"],"terms":[{"t":"Cathaeans","d":"An independent Indian tribe reputed very daring and skilled in war, who held Sangala."}]},{"i":210,"j":214,"loc":"Book V, 25–29","title":"The mutiny at the Hyphasis","summary":"Reports of rich, well-governed lands beyond the Hyphasis fire Alexander, but his men's spirit flags and they talk of refusing to go on. He addresses his officers, listing their conquests and arguing that the outer sea is near, that unconquered peoples would stir revolt, and that glory comes through labour, as with Heracles and Dionysus. Coenus replies for the army: few of the original men are left, they are worn out and long for home, and Alexander should return and start a new expedition with fresh troops. After three days sulking in his tent and unfavourable sacrifices, Alexander announces the return, sets up twelve great altars, and marches back to the Hydaspes.","ideas":["Alexander: no end to labours for a brave man but the labours themselves","He claims the Great Sea encircles the whole earth","Coenus: self-control in success is the noblest virtue","The army rejoices that Alexander let himself be conquered by them alone"],"terms":[{"t":"Hyphasis","d":"The easternmost river Alexander reached; his army refused to cross it."}]},{"i":215,"j":220,"loc":"Book VI, 1–5","title":"The fleet sails down the Hydaspes","summary":"Alexander first thinks the Indus is the upper Nile because of its crocodiles and beans, then learns better and deletes the claim from his letter to Olympias. Coenus dies; Porus is made king of the conquered region, and the army divides between the fleet under Nearchus and columns under Craterus and Hephaestion on both banks. After libations to the rivers, Heracles and Ammon, nearly 2,000 vessels set out with a roar of oars that draws crowds of singing Indians. At the confluence of the Hydaspes and Acesines the eddies wreck several long warships, and Alexander arranges his forces in staggered divisions to trap the Mallians.","ideas":["A mistaken guess about the source of the Nile is withdrawn","Arrian says Onesicritus falsely called himself admiral","Round merchant ships survive the eddies; long warships do not","Forces are staggered so fugitives run into another division"],"terms":[{"t":"Nearchus","d":"Commander of the whole fleet on the river voyage and afterwards on the ocean."}]},{"i":221,"j":224,"loc":"Book VI, 6–9","title":"The Mallian campaign and the leap","summary":"Alexander marches through waterless country to surprise the Mallians, killing many unarmed outside their city and storming its citadel, while Perdiccas chases fugitives elsewhere. He presses across the Hydraotes, takes a Brahmin city where he is first on the wall, and pursues some 50,000 Mallians with cavalry alone until his infantry arrive. At the largest Mallian city he grows impatient with the ladder-bearers, climbs a ladder himself with Peucestas, Leonnatus and Abreas, and the ladder breaks behind them. Exposed on the wall, he chooses to leap down inside the citadel and fights alone with his back to the wall.","ideas":["A route thought impassable is chosen to achieve surprise","Alexander's example shames his men up the ladders","The guards' eagerness breaks the ladder","Alexander reasons that dying after a great deed is not ignoble"],"terms":[{"t":"Mallians","d":"A numerous, warlike independent Indian tribe that planned to join the Oxydracians against Alexander."},{"t":"Brachmans","d":"The Brahmins, whom Arrian calls the philosophers of the Indians."}]},{"i":225,"j":229,"loc":"Book VI, 10–14","title":"Alexander's wound and his return to the army","summary":"Abreas is killed and Alexander is shot through the breastplate into the chest; Peucestas shields him with the sacred shield from Troy until the Macedonians break in and kill everyone in the city. Arrian weighs reports of how the arrow was removed and corrects common errors: the wound happened among the Mallians, the last battle was at Gaugamela not Arbela, and Ptolemy was not even present. The army, told he is dead, despairs and disbelieves his letters until he sails into camp, raises his hand, and mounts his horse. Friends rebuke his recklessness; the Mallians and Oxydracians submit, and the fleet sails on to the Indus.","ideas":["Rumour keeps first falsehoods alive unless history corrects them","The army fears it is leaderless among hostile nations","Arrian judges Alexander lacked the self-control to avoid danger","An old Boeotian says doers of great deeds must suffer"],"terms":[{"t":"Soter","d":"'Preserver', a title some writers wrongly said Ptolemy earned by shielding Alexander."}]},{"i":230,"j":235,"loc":"Book VI, 15–20","title":"Down the Indus to the outer sea","summary":"At the Indus confluence Alexander founds a city and dockyard, makes Oxyartes a viceroy, and sends Craterus by land toward Arachosia. He forces the submission of Musicanus, captures Oxycanus, receives Sambus's capital, and later has the rebel Musicanus and the Brahmins who incited him hanged. Patala is found deserted and resettled; sailing down the right branch without pilots, the fleet is damaged by storm and then stranded and battered by the tide, which the Macedonians had never seen. Alexander sacrifices on the islands as Ammon directed, sails into the open ocean to offer bulls to Poseidon, and later explores the western mouth as easier to navigate.","ideas":["Prompt surrender wins pardon, revolt brings hanging","The tide's ebb and flow alarms the inexperienced Macedonians","Arrian thinks the ocean voyage was chiefly for the boast","Harbours, wells and dockyards are prepared for the fleet"],"terms":[{"t":"Patala","d":"The Indus delta region and city where the river splits into two branches."}]},{"i":236,"j":241,"loc":"Book VI, 21–26","title":"Through Ora and the Gadrosian desert","summary":"While Nearchus waits for the monsoon to change, Alexander attacks the Oritians, founds a city at Rhambacia, and leaves Leonnatus in Ora. He enters the Gadrosian desert, where Aristobulus describes myrrh, nard and sea-washed trees; Nearchus says Alexander chose the route to outdo Semiramis and Cyrus. Heat, deep sand, long night marches and thirst destroy men and animals; soldiers secretly eat the pack beasts, the sick are left behind, and a flash flood sweeps away women, children and baggage. Alexander pours away a helmet of water brought to him, which Arrian praises above all his deeds, and when the guides lose the way he finds water by the sea.","ideas":["Rivalry with Semiramis and Cyrus motivates the route","Hunger leads the guards to eat the sealed grain, and Alexander pardons them","Alexander pretends not to know the beasts are being eaten","Pouring out the water shows endurance and command"],"terms":[{"t":"Gadrosia","d":"The arid region west of the Indus whose crossing cost the army more than all its Asian hardships."}]},{"i":242,"j":246,"loc":"Book VI, 27–30","title":"Carmania, punishments, and the tomb of Cyrus","summary":"At the Gadrosian capital and in Carmania Alexander deposes Apollophanes, learns of Philip's murder in India, and executes Cleander and Sitalces for plundering temples and tombs, which Arrian sees as how he held his empire together. Arrian rejects the story of a Dionysiac revel through Carmania, records the thank-offerings and contests, and Peucestas's elevation to eighth bodyguard. Nearchus reports his voyage and is sent on to the Tigris. At Pasargadae Alexander finds the tomb of Cyrus looted and has Aristobulus restore it; he hangs Orxines and appoints Peucestas, who adopts Persian dress and speech, as viceroy of Persis.","ideas":["Rulers who wrong their subjects are punished as a warning","Arrian dismisses the Bacchic procession as unsupported by good sources","The plundered tomb of Cyrus is restored and sealed","Peucestas wins Persian favour by adopting their customs"],"terms":[{"t":"Thriambus","d":"A name of Dionysus, said to underlie the word for victory processions."},{"t":"Pasargadae","d":"The Persian royal city where the tomb of Cyrus stood in a watered park."}]},{"i":247,"j":254,"loc":"Book VII, 1–6","title":"Philosophers, Calanus, and the Susa weddings","summary":"Arrian reflects on Alexander's boundless plans, saying he would have striven with himself had no rival remained, and contrasts the Indian sages who tell him he will soon own only a grave, Diogenes, and Dandamis, who refuses to join him. Calanus, who did join him, falls ill in Persis and burns himself on a pyre without moving, to the army's war-cry. At Susa Alexander punishes misgoverning satraps, marries Barsine and Parysatis, and gives Persian and Median noblewomen to some eighty Companions. He pays the soldiers' debts without recording names, crowns Peucestas, Leonnatus and Nearchus, and receives 30,000 Asian youths trained as Macedonians, which with his Persian ways offends the Macedonians.","ideas":["Alexander praises the sages' words but acts against them","Calanus shows how steadfast the human mind can be","Mass marriages are held in the Persian manner","Foreigners enrolled in elite units breed Macedonian resentment"],"terms":[{"t":"Epigoni","d":"'Successors', the 30,000 young Asians armed and drilled in the Macedonian style."},{"t":"Calanus","d":"An Indian philosopher who followed Alexander and chose death by fire when ill."}]},{"i":255,"j":260,"loc":"Book VII, 7–12","title":"The mutiny at Opis and the reconciliation","summary":"Alexander sails the Eulaeus and the Persian Gulf, goes up the Tigris, and tears down the Persian weirs as unworthy of victors. At Opis his plan to discharge the unfit enrages the Macedonians, who tell him to campaign with his father Ammon; he has thirteen ringleaders executed and delivers a speech recalling what Philip and he made of them, his own wounds and modest life, and telling them all to go home. He shuts himself away and gives commands to Persians, until the Macedonians throw down their arms at his gates; when Callines complains that only Persians may kiss him, Alexander calls them all kinsmen. A banquet prays for harmony between Macedonians and Persians, and about 10,000 veterans leave under Craterus, who is to replace Antipater.","ideas":["Philip made shepherds in hides into rulers","Alexander claims to keep only the purple robe and diadem","The kiss of kinsmen ends the mutiny","Arrian rejects the view that Antipater was being disgraced"],"terms":[{"t":"Opis","d":"A city on the Tigris where the army mutinied over the discharge of veterans."}]},{"i":261,"j":264,"loc":"Book VII, 13–16","title":"Hephaestion's death and the embassies","summary":"On the journey to Ecbatana Alexander sees the Nisaean horse plain and, by one story, is shown Amazon horsewomen, which Arrian doubts while not dismissing the Amazons as fiction. At Ecbatana Hephaestion dies of a fever; Arrian sorts the conflicting reports of Alexander's grief, accepting that he fasted for three days, ordered a costly pyre and public mourning, and kept Hephaestion's name on his cavalry division. Alexander then subdues the mountain Cossaeans in winter and receives embassies from Libya, Italy and peoples of the far west; Arrian doubts the reported Roman embassy. He sends shipwrights to Hyrcania to explore the Caspian.","ideas":["Writers' accounts of the grief reflect their goodwill or envy","Cutting his hair recalls Achilles","Envoys from distant nations make Alexander seem lord of land and sea","The Caspian's link to other seas is still unknown"],"terms":[{"t":"Cossaeans","d":"Raiding mountaineers near the Uxians whom Alexander subdued in winter."}]},{"i":265,"j":271,"loc":"Book VII, 16–21","title":"Babylon: warnings, fleets, and plans for Arabia","summary":"The Chaldaeans warn Alexander, by an oracle of Belus, not to enter Babylon, or at least not facing west; he suspects they fear losing the temple revenues and the marshes defeat his attempt to go round. Aristobulus tells how the diviner Peithagoras found lobeless livers for Hephaestion and Alexander, and Calanus is recalled as saying he would greet Alexander at Babylon. Alexander returns Greek statues Xerxes took, gathers fleets and digs a vast harbour, and plans to conquer Arabia, which Arrian puts down to insatiable ambition; scouts report on the islands and the great peninsula. He then sails to the Pallacopas canal to regulate the Euphrates flood and founds a city there.","ideas":["Arrian thinks dying at the height of glory may have been better","Omens and prophecies point to Alexander's end","Arabia is coveted for its spices and long coastline","The Euphrates must be kept from draining into the marshes"],"terms":[{"t":"Belus","d":"The Babylonian god whose great temple Xerxes destroyed and Alexander meant to rebuild."},{"t":"Pallacopas","d":"A canal off the Euphrates carrying its floodwater into the marshes."}]},{"i":272,"j":279,"loc":"Book VII, 22–26","title":"Last omens and the death of Alexander","summary":"Sailing the marshes past old Assyrian royal tombs, Alexander loses his hat and fillet, which a sailor retrieves by wearing it on his head, an omen variously told. Back in Babylon he mixes Persians into Macedonian companies, learns that Ammon allows Hephaestion hero-cult, and writes to Cleomenes a promise of pardon that Arrian condemns. A stranger sits on the empty throne, and after a long night of drinking with Medius Alexander falls ill. Following the Royal Diary, Arrian traces his daily baths, sacrifices and orders for the Arabian voyage as the fever worsens, until he can no longer speak, greets his soldiers with his eyes, and dies after the Serapis oracle tells his friends to leave him where he is.","ideas":["The fillet incident is read as an omen for Alexander and Seleucus","Mixed companies of twelve Persians and four Macedonians","Arrian blames the pledge of impunity to the wicked Cleomenes","To the last Alexander plans the expedition"],"terms":[{"t":"Royal Diary","d":"The court journal Arrian follows for the course of Alexander's last illness."}]},{"i":280,"j":283,"loc":"Book VII, 27–30","title":"Rumours of poison and Arrian's verdict","summary":"Arrian records, without believing them, the stories that Antipater sent poison, procured by Aristotle and given by Iollas, and that Alexander tried to vanish into the Euphrates to seem divine. He gives Alexander's age and reign and a portrait of his qualities: courage, piety, self-control in bodily pleasures, insatiable only for praise, a gifted general, faithful to agreements and generous. He excuses his errors by youth, good fortune and flatterers, praises him as unique among kings in repenting, and treats his divine descent and Persian dress as political devices. He ends by telling critics to weigh all Alexander's deeds against their own small lives, and says he wrote the history not without divine inspiration.","ideas":["Poison stories are recorded only to show he knows them","Repentance is the only cure for wrongdoing","Persian dress meant to reconcile foreigners and check Macedonian insolence","Arrian censures some acts but admires the man"],"terms":[]}]});